@prompt
{{no_editor}}

This tutorial introduces both the Hazel language and editor, and in particular, programming with live probes. Click the video below for a teaser demo. 

{{video:hazel-probes-intro.mp4}}

On each slide, read the preamble, expand the *task* panel and try what it suggests. Move between slides with the arrows at the bottom of each slide, or use the tutorial menu above the editor to jump directly between slides.

This tutorial is loosely themed around an imaginary farming sim game, **Night Gardener**. Don't worry too much about understanding the domain details of the code samples and tasks... the premise here is that you've been dropped into an unfamiliar codebase, and probes are there to help you find your footing.

- Some slides ask you to complete a task. A **🤔** face shows at the bottom while your code is incomplete or incorrect, becoming a **🤩** face once it passes. Other slides just invite you to explore or try something out.

- Alternatively, simply poke around and explore on your own. Your work is saved as you go: refreshing or accidentally closing the tab won't lose your progress.

At the end there are a few more substantial program-writing and debugging tasks to exercise what you've learned.

@reference
### Task Reference
This sidebar is the **Task Reference**. As the tutorial introduces probe commands, a quick reference and a few controls will appear here for the current slide.

